      "categoryName": "Consonant Clusters റ്റ / ന്റ",
      "title": "Consonant Clusters: റ്റ and ന്റ",
      "type": "introduction",
      "completed": false,
      "section": 4,
      "content": {
        "steps": [
          {
            "heading": "Two essential consonant clusters",
            "characters": [
              { "malayalam": "റ്റ", "iso15919": "ṟṟa", "description": "റ + ് + റ — the strong alveolar trill doubled. Pronounced as a geminate retroflex stop /ṭṭ/. Extremely common in everyday Malayalam." },
              { "malayalam": "ന്റ", "iso15919": "nṟa", "description": "ന + ് + റ — alveolar nasal joined to the alveolar trill. Pronounced /nda/ or /nṟ/. One of the most frequent word-final clusters in Malayalam." }
            ],
            "matchingExercise": [
              { "left": "റ + റ", "right": "റ്റ  (ṟṟa)", "desc": "trill + virama + trill" },
              { "left": "ന + റ", "right": "ന്റ  (nṟa)", "desc": "nasal + virama + trill" }
            ]
          }
        ],
        "insight": "Both ligatures are built on Malayalam's strong alveolar trill റ (ṟa), which you learned in Module 6. The virama joins them to the preceding consonant — the same mechanism as all other ligatures you have learned. These two forms appear in hundreds of common Malayalam words."
      }
    },
